WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
When should a government agency use focus
groups?

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:06.320
Picture a draft public service as a map.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.580
A survey may show how many people chose a
route.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.660
A focus group can reveal why the signs
confused them.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.920
Use government focus groups when you need
people to discuss a shared issue,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.240
compare experiences or explain how they
understand a proposed change.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.320
Begin with the public decision,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.760
then decide who needs to be heard and what
participants can actually

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.300
influence.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.760
For example, an agency considering a new
service notice could ask

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.700
participants what the notice says in their
own words, what is unclear and

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.080
what information is missing.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.480
Those answers can guide a revision.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.560
They do not estimate the views of all
residents.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.720
Plan access, consent and reporting before
invitations go out.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.240
PRC Impact helps public organizations
scope and lead qualitative research.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.860
Discuss the decision at prcimpact.com, or
call

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.260
334-429-6994.
